  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nThrough Friday: Continued dry and cooler. Mid afternoon surface \nanalysis had a nearly stationary front well to our southeast and a \ncold front over the mid Mississippi river valley with a weak surface \nhigh in between. Mid afternoon satellite imagery/RAP analysis showed \na shortwave trough over the central Plains that will continue \nswinging east tonight and help send the cold front towards our CWA. \nUnder clear skies and a near calm wind strong radiational cooling \nwill lead to a cooler night where most sights will bottom out in the \n40s. The cold front will move through the CWA as a dry cold front. \nDue to its timing, most sites will top out warmer than normal in the \n70s. /22/\n\nThrough mid next week (Wednesday)...As the upper trough phases over \nthe Great Plains into the upper low, it will swing east into the \nweekend, with subtle shortwave ridging moving in by late weekend. \nThis will be in advance of an upper low over the Rio Grande Valley \ninto central Mexico, with increased isentropic ascent and clouds \ninto the weekend. 1025mb surface high will build in late weekend \ninto the Mid South, bringing much cooler temperatures (low to upper \n60s north of Interstate 20 Saturday and Sunday, while low 70s \nSaturday and mid to upper 60s Sunday). Seasonably cool lows, some 5F \nto 10F below, will be the norm (39F to 50F Friday night and 35F to \n42F Saturday and Sunday nights). Patchy frost may be a concern \nacross the region Saturday and Sunday nights. There will be some \ngradient wind Saturday night and more calm Sunday night, but winds \nshould remain light enough to support some frost potential, \nespecially in low-lying and sheltered areas. Now is a good time to \nensure plants, pets, and people are considered with more routine \nlows nearing frost to light freeze potential. Considering adding a \nLimited graphic in HWO for frost during these nights, mainly in \nnorth to northeast MS.\n\nEarly to mid next week: Quiet, partly cloudy and mostly dry pattern \nwill persist into the following week. Upper low parked over west-\ncentral TX will eject eastward, keeping moist ascent across the Gulf \nCoast states. Some clouds will persist during that time and cannot \nrule out isolated rain shower during this time.\nWith some thermal temps warming, highs will moderate from seasonable \nearly week to seasonably warm mid week (65F to 75F Monday to 70F to \n78F by Wednesday). Lows will remain seasonably cool but gradually \nless cold. /DC/",
